# Florent Bot — Project Context

## Product
An autonomous competitive bot for the Florent Code League: two teams of
robots battle on a grid to mine titanium and destroy the enemy core. Win
by better tiebreakers at round 1000 (titanium delivered to core →
harvesters alive → titanium stored) or by destroying the enemy Core
outright. Every feature exists to serve one of those two paths — harvest
economy or combat — nothing else.

## Architecture
- Language: Python only. Entry point: top-level `class Player` with
  `run(self, ct: Controller) -> None` in `main.py`.
- Engine: `run()` is called once per round for every living unit. 10ms CPU
  budget per unit per round. Any uncaught exception besides the engine's
  own timeout permanently destroys that unit for the rest of the match.
- No shared game object — all state is read through the unit-scoped
  `Controller` (`ct`) passed into `run()`. Cross-unit/cross-round state
  only survives via the 16-int comm store (per team, one-round write lag)
  or module-level Python state (verify this persists across rounds in
  this engine's process model before relying on it for anything
  correctness-critical).

## 5 Pieces (Never Break These Boundaries)
1. `main.py` — entry point only: dispatch by `ct.get_entity_type()`, call
   into the pieces below. No decision logic lives here.
2. `economy.py` — harvesting: explore, claim ore, build conveyor +
   harvester. Called from `builder_role.py`, not a role itself.
3. `threat.py` — `is_core_threatened(ct) -> bool`, recomputed fresh every
   round, no latched state.
4. `roles/` — one file per `EntityType` (`core_role.py`,
   `builder_role.py`, `gunner_role.py`). Only files under `roles/` may
   call mutating `ct.*` methods (`move`, `build_*`, `fire`, `heal`,
   `destroy`, `rotate`, `launch`, `spawn_builder`, `convert_ammo`,
   `write_store`, `self_destruct`, `resign`).
5. `strategy.py` — economy-sufficient check, attack trigger, finish-core
   commit state with an explicit exit condition.

Roles read from `economy.py`/`threat.py`/`strategy.py`; those modules never
call into `roles/`. Keep the dependency direction one-way.

## Shared Conventions
- Costs: always `ct.get_<entity>_cost()`, never a hardcoded base cost —
  cost scales with what's already been built.
- Positions: `Position(x, y)`, cardinal-only builder movement via
  `pos.cardinal_direction_to(target)`, never `pos.direction_to(target)`.
- Build/attack/heal/destroy targets: orthogonally adjacent tile only, not
  diagonal, not the unit's own tile.
- Every mutating call is `can_*()`-gated first — the engine raises
  `GameError` rather than no-opping.
- Every `run()` / role `execute()` wrapped in try/except so nothing but
  the engine's own timeout can escape.


## Testing & Benchmarking Loop
Every phase closes with a benchmark run, not just passing unit tests.
1. Run the fast test suite first (`pytest tests/ -q`) — 0 failures before
   anything else.
2. Run the benchmark suite (or the agreed fast subset for a given phase —
   named per-phase in `docs/PROGRESS_TRACKER.md`) and record the actual
   numbers: titanium mined, maps mining 0, GameError count, win rate vs.
   the starter bot where relevant.
3. Compare against the previous checkpoint's recorded numbers in
   `docs/PROGRESS_TRACKER.md` — a phase is only checkpointed if it matches
   or improves on the prior baseline. A regression means bisect which
   change caused it before moving on; don't guess.
4. Record results directly as plain text in `docs/PROGRESS_TRACKER.md`'s
   existing fields. One markdown file, filled in by hand after a human (me) has
   watched the relevant replay and confirmed it.
5. Checkboxes in `docs/PROGRESS_TRACKER.md` only get checked after I
   explicitly confirm a replay — never check a box based on test/benchmark
   output alone, since a passing benchmark doesn't guarantee correct
   in-game behavior (e.g. it can still mine 0 while "tests pass").

## Session Hygiene
- Start of session: state which phase and checkpoint we're resuming from,
  per `docs/PROGRESS_TRACKER.md`.
- End of session: update the tracker's checkboxes (only the ones I've
  confirmed) and fill in every blank field touched (thresholds, radii,
  benchmark numbers).
- If a phase fails its gate and gets reverted, log what was tried and why
  it didn't pass — don't silently discard it or re-attempt the same
  approach later without noting it failed once already.

